### Annual Anomalies  

Because the climate normal on the coast is upwards of 60 inches, and it looks like the coast was in the mid-50s range in 2025, which is a little drier than normal. Climate normals in the northern part of the watershed are in the mid-to-upper 50s so they look about typical for 2025. I'm eyeballing here and the color scale is slightly different, but see the [MSEP Characterization chapter on precipitation](https://msepcharacterization.netlify.app/precipitation) for the details I have compiled in the past (climate normals from NCEI).  

#### Map  


::: {.cell}

:::



::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![Map of 2025 differences from 30-year average precipitation, in inches, averaged to the HUC-8 (subbasin) level. Darker colors indicate totals further from the long-term average.](precipitation_files/figure-pdf/fig-mapPrcpAnnualAnoms-1.pdf){#fig-mapPrcpAnnualAnoms}
:::
:::



#### Time Series Graph  


::: {.cell}

:::




::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![Time series of annual anomalies (difference between that year and the 30-year climate normal, in inches) by HUC-8 (subbasin). Each line represents a subbasin.](precipitation_files/figure-pdf/fig-tsAnnualAnoms-1.pdf){#fig-tsAnnualAnoms}
:::
:::


The y-axis may not be that meaningful if you don't already know that the annual normal amount is around 60 inches. So let's look at the difference as a percent. 

From this 20-year time series, we can see that generally things tend to be similar (compared to normal) across all the HUC8s in the watershed. Some years have more variability, like 2019 and 2020. We had a fairly long stretch from 2012-2021 or so where it generally was normal to wetter-than-normal. 2025 was generally drier than normal across the watershed; drier in some HUC8s than others. As seen from the map, the southern counties were the ones that generally were much drier than normal.  


